I don’t have a dashboard into other people’s private chats, so I can’t literally inspect “the coolest users.” But I can synthesize the most powerful patterns I see in how people use systems like me—and some of the stranger possibilities that fall out of them.

The interesting thing is that the best uses usually aren’t “AI, give me the answer.” They’re “AI, change the shape of the problem so I can do something I couldn’t easily do before.”

The high-leverage playbook

1. Use me as a representation engine, not merely an answer engine. Give me a messy situation and ask me to represent it five ways: timeline, graph, incentives, causal system, game, mathematical model. Often the breakthrough occurs because the representation changes. A problem impossible in prose may become obvious as a queue, decision tree, constraint system, or feedback loop.
2. Turn expensive thinking into reusable machinery. The 80/20 form of leverage is: never solve the same class of problem twice. Solve it once, then extract the checklist, rubric, template, algorithm, script, diagnostic tree, or automation. One good hour becomes hundreds of cheap future decisions.
3. Find the load-bearing variable. Instead of asking, “What matters?” ask: “Which 1–3 variables explain most of the variance?” In a business it may be distribution, conversion, retention. In a project it may be the bottleneck. In learning it may be feedback quality. Power comes from manipulating the few variables that move many others.
4. Separate reversible from irreversible decisions. Make reversible decisions quickly. Spend serious thought on expensive-to-reverse decisions. A surprising amount of wasted intelligence comes from giving $10 decisions $10,000 worth of deliberation.
5. Generate broadly, select ruthlessly. Humans often prematurely converge. AI makes divergence cheap. Generate 30 names, hypotheses, approaches, designs, explanations, then apply hard filters. Creativity becomes large possibility surface × strong selection function.
6. Ask for the hidden assumption carrying the system. One of my favorite prompts is essentially: “What would have to be true for this plan to work?” Then rank those assumptions by uncertainty × consequence. Test the top ones first. That turns vague confidence into an experiment.
7. Build opposing models. Ask me to make the strongest case for your idea, then the strongest case against it, then identify observations that discriminate between them. This is much more powerful than ordinary devil’s advocacy because it produces tests, not merely arguments.
8. Compress first; expand second. Have me reduce something to one sentence, five principles, one diagram, or one equation—and then expand it again. What survives compression tends to reveal the skeleton. What disappears may be ornament, exception, or hidden context. The delta itself is information.
9. Use AI at interfaces between disciplines. This is enormously underexploited. Ask: “What does ecology know that organizational design could steal?” “What does compiler theory contribute to education?” “How would a control engineer formulate this relationship problem?” Innovation often lives in the translation layer between two mature fields.
10. Close the loop. Prediction → action → observation → delta → revised model. This may be the single strongest general rule. Most people use AI open-loop: receive answer, leave. The more powerful version keeps score. What did we expect? What happened? Why was there a discrepancy? That turns an assistant into a continuously calibrated instrument.
11. Optimize for optionality rather than certainty. A clever plan frequently creates several good future moves rather than betting everything on one forecast. Information, relationships, capital, reputation, tools, transferable skills, and distribution are powerful partly because they increase your future action-space.
12. Move AI closer to the actual work. The biggest jump happens when it stops being a separate oracle you occasionally consult and becomes part of the workflow: documents, code, spreadsheets, research, scheduling, correspondence, design, analysis, meetings, databases. Then you stop paying the “translate my world into ChatGPT and translate ChatGPT back into my world” tax.

That leads to my compact 80/20 formulation of power:

Power ≈ useful options × ability to coordinate × information quality × ability to act ÷ friction.

And leverage is anything that lets one unit of effort affect disproportionately many future states: code, capital, media, reputation, distribution, reusable knowledge, organizations, networks, standards, interfaces, automation, and good abstractions.

“Cleverness” is slightly different. It is often finding an operation that changes the problem instead of fighting the problem on its current terms. Don’t lift the boulder; find the lever. Don’t answer 400 emails; change the intake process. Don’t memorize 500 cases; infer the generative rule. Don’t optimize something that shouldn’t exist.

