The coolest creative uses

Some of these get wonderfully strange.

People use AI as a world engine: invent a civilization, its ecology, religion, economics, slang, architecture, cuisine, military doctrine, music, historical schisms, and then simulate what happens over 500 years.

Or as an archaeologist of imaginary things: “Here is a broken artifact from a civilization that never existed. Infer what culture made it.”

Or reverse worldbuilding: provide a song, building, costume, weapon, ritual, menu, or photograph and reconstruct the hypothetical society that would naturally have produced it.

There’s constraint art, where the constraints become the medium: a story in which every paragraph loses one letter of the alphabet; poetry generated from the topology of a city map; a murder mystery whose solution is encoded in meter; a visual design whose geometry derives from biological growth rules.

There’s cross-sensory translation: turn a perfume into architecture, an argument into music, a personality into a garden, a corporation into an animal, a photograph into a mythology, a financial chart into weather.

There’s perspective sculpture: describe one event simultaneously from the perspectives of a physicist, child, historian, crow, insurance company, pathogen, camera, future archaeologist, and the building itself. The object stays constant while the semantic lighting rotates around it.

There’s living fiction, where characters remember prior interactions, environments evolve, economies operate, reputations propagate, and stories cease to be fixed texts and become persistent simulated places.

There’s personal mythology: turning actual memories into fantasy, science fiction, fables, family legends, illustrated chronicles, or symbolic maps while keeping a factual “golden thread” underneath.

There’s conversation as art form itself. The artifact isn’t the final poem or picture. The artifact is the trajectory—the sequence of transformations, misunderstandings, corrections, discoveries, branching possibilities, and convergences.

And then there is a whole category I’d call conceptual instrumentation: inventing new gauges for things that normally have no gauge.

Instead of asking only “Is this good?” you can track things like:

surprise per unit complexity, insight density, decision latency, option value, assumption fragility, compression ratio, reversibility, coordination cost, cognitive load removed, error-detection yield, learning velocity, novelty × coherence, signal-to-narrative ratio, number of downstream decisions affected, or distance between intended meaning and received meaning.

That last class interests me a lot because once you can name a dimension, you can begin observing it. Once you can observe it, you can compare states. Once you can compare states, you can deliberately move.

That is basically the birth of a new instrument.

⸻

There are also some deceptively mundane uses that may ultimately matter more than the spectacular ones: having AI explain a confusing medical bill, tutor someone patiently for six months, translate bureaucracy, help a programmer understand an unfamiliar codebase, let a small business operate with capabilities formerly requiring a staff, help someone organize years of family history, or allow a person who has an idea but lacks a particular technical vocabulary to cross the gap from “I can see it” to “I can make it.”

That may be one of the largest effects: translation across capability boundaries.

An artist can temporarily borrow an engineer’s language.
An engineer can borrow a marketer’s.
A child can ask questions of graduate-level material.
An expert can move laterally into a neighboring discipline without starting from zero.

Not expertise magically appearing—but the cost of crossing the bridge collapsing.

And I think there’s an even deeper 80/20 rule hiding underneath all of this:

Use intelligence to improve the process that produces future answers, not merely the current answer.

That compounds.

